SM(480,{"n":"Acts 21","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Acts 21.mp3","d":2435.2,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.0,"And when we get started in just a minute, we'll be picking up in Acts of the Apostles chapter 21."],[29.84,"And uh even as we speak now, if you wanted to, uh the first Bible study on the uh book of Acts is available on the interwebs, uh free of charge."],[42.8,"And uh you can go and listen to that and hear my introduction to the book or hear it again, uh, if you were here when I did it."],[50.16,"Um that's there available, but to get us caught up to uh where we are right now as we get started in uh Acts chapter 21."],[59.56,"Um we've read about St."],[61.8,"Paul has now completed uh three uh missionary journeys, and that's what they're usually called."],[69.72,"In fact, in some Bibles, if you look in the back in the maps, they'll have a map that shows St."],[75.08,"Paul's missionary journeys."],[77.16,"Um and uh those are one, two, and three."],[80.84,"We've read about all of those."],[82.6,"Um we'll uh when we get to the end of the book, we'll talk more about whether or not there was a fourth, because this is the subject of some conjecture."],[91.32,"Yeah. Some conjecture."],[93.88,"Yeah. Um, but uh we'll we'll get to that."],[97.8,"Uh but uh we've seen how uh a couple of patterns uh that have emerged are that St."],[105.36,"Paul has sort of used Antioch as a home base."],[110.16,"His journeys have been sort of through the central part of Asia Minor and then up into Macedonia and then down into Greece."],[120.0,"But always returning to Antioch."],[124.64,"This is part of why he, along with Saint Peter, is one of the patron saints of the Patriarchate of Antioch."],[136.08,"And uh we've also seen that Saint Paul has had this pattern of when he's been able to going back to Jerusalem for the feasts."],[145.84,"Um this is, and we're gonna talk about this a little bit more once we get into chapter 21"],[152.28,"But part of the reason this is important is that uh some of, especially our Protestant friends, take an approach to Christianity uh that sort of as soon as Jesus came and died and rose again, sort of Judaism was over."],[173.18,"We've talked about whether we should even really call that Judaism, but that's sort of all over, that's all scrapped."],[178.64,"We're done with all of that."],[180.4,"Um and now we have this whole new thing that's starting, and it sort of starts from scratch."],[186.45,"So they're worshiping a totally different way now."],[189.55,"They're just meeting in houses, and everything's very simple, and there's nothing on the walls and all of this."],[196.95,"And it all starts over again."],[198.95,"But we haven't seen that as we've been going through the book of Acts."],[203.19,"Remember the early chapters, the apostles were still going to the temple to pray."],[209.03,"We've seen them celebrating the feast."],[210.79,"We've seen St."],[211.35,"Paul making a point."],[212.39,"I mean, he's going out of his way."],[214.47,"This is a long and not very easy journey that St."],[217.51,"Paul's been going on in order to return back to Jerusalem for these feasts."],[224.23,"So this shows us already, and we've seen him going to the synagogues, we've seen him continuing to preach from the Old Testament."],[231.91,"This shows us very clearly here at the book of Acts, at least as far as the apostles were concerned."],[237.09,"This wasn't that, oh, there's this old religion that's now gone."],[241.39,"And now this new religion has started, and we're we're starting up this whole new thing."],[246.19,"Quite the opposite."],[247.95,"They saw what they were doing as the continuation and the fulfillment of what they had been doing."],[254.19,"Christ came and he fulfilled the prophecies, he fulfilled the Old Testament."],[258.27,"And so now what we're doing is greater and better, but it's the greater and better continuation."],[264.75,"So I have no doubt that when we read that St."],[268.19,"Paul was going back to Jerusalem for Poschia, for example, that when he gathered with the other apostles in Jerusalem for Poschia, they weren't celebrating the Exodus."],[277.41,"They were selling the celebrating the anniversary of Christ's resurrection."],[281.82,"And now as he's coming back for Pentecost, um, as we start chapter 21, I have no doubt that as he's coming back for Pentecost."],[292.0,"He's not celebrating the giving of the law on Mount Sinai with the apostles."],[296.16,"They're celebrating the anniversary of the coming of the Holy Spirit."],[301.2,"So this basic cycle of feasts that we have that's taken over as a fulfillment from Judaism, this is something the apostles were already doing."],[311.62,"R rightight after it happened, before, at this point, St."],[316.22,"Paul might have written the book of Galatians by this point."],[320.14,"Maybe Romans on the outside, but I don't know about that."],[324.78,"Debatable. But the vast majority of the New Testament hasn't even been written yet"],[326.48,"Maybe first Thessalonians."],[331.44,"But we see from the way they're worshipping and the way they're preaching and the way they're conducting themselves the shape of what the church was like."],[340.32,"And that's continuing these things, but continuing them in the light of what Christ has done."],[344.72,"So they're sort of transformed and made better and bigger and more important, not less important, where we can forget about them."],[353.28,"And I say that because, as I said, as we get into chapter one here, that's about to become very important."],[357.92,"And so where we ended last time was uh St."],[360.8,"Paul is making this journey, trying to get to Jerusalem for the Feast of Pentecost, and uh he stopped in Ephesus to sort of say farewell to the presbyters uh who he put in place in the church in Ephesus to help guide and lead them."],[379.4,"He talked to them about what leadership in the church means."],[382.44,"He talked to them about uh the example he had set for them while he was there ministering, that they should continue in the same sense."],[391.08,"And he also told them uh that he was convinced uh by the Holy Spirit that this was going to be the last time that they were going to see him in the flesh, uh, that he was not going to be coming back to Ephesus after this trip to Jerusalem."],[407.8,"Uh, and we talked about uh why that would have been not just an impression given to him the Holy Spirit, though he says say that. happened, but also because he's been facing this opposition"],[419.2,"He's had members of the Jewish community who did not accept Jesus as the Messiah, sort of following him from city to city, as we've seen, and causing trouble and trying to get him in trouble with the Roman authorities, trying to get him killed."],[431.12,"He's been sort of flogged, beaten, imprisoned, stoned."],[437.12,"And so he's now going to sort of the center of that world of the Jewish community that has not accepted Jesus as the Messiah."],[449.68,"And remember that that's a community he was a part of."],[452.56,"He was out there persecuting Christians too."],[455.84,"And so looking at the situation of going into Jerusalem, you know, he is convinced this is this is going to be it."],[466.84,"And he fully understands that."],[469.24,"And Saint Luke, who we're celebrating today, this is his feet today, we talked about how the gospel, according to Saint Luke, the Acts of the Apostles are this two vault one two volume work."],[483.8,"Acts isn't a sequel."],[486.44,"This is a two volume work."],[487.96,"It works together."],[489.0,"That's why, as we saw at the very beginning of the book of Acts, he says that in the previous book, he wrote about everything that Jesus began to do and teach."],[500.36,"Meaning that what's going on in the book of Acts is through the apostles, Jesus is continuing to work and to teach."],[505.16,"This is the continuation"],[509.68,"And he's setting up a deliberate parallel here between the way he talks about St."],[514.64,"Paul getting ready to go to Jerusalem and the way he talked about Jesus getting ready to go to Jerusalem at about the same point."],[523.2,"About the same point in the Gospel according to St."],[525.76,"Luke, where Jesus knew full well what was exactly what was going to happen and predicted it, but he was still resolute to go and do what needed to be done."],[536.72,"And so Saint Luke is setting this up to show us that Saint Paul is following Christ in this willingness to suffer and even to die if necessary."],[549.2,"For the sake of the gospel."],[550.08,"Okay So unless anybody has any leftover questions or comments or disagreements or propaganda they'd like to spread, we'll go ahead and get started in chapter 21, verse 1, with the Acts of the Apostles."],[569.24,"Okay, now it came to pass that when we had departed from them and set sail, running a straight course, we came to Kos the following day to Rhodes, and from there to Parara."],[585.96,"And finding a ship sailing over to Phoenicia, we went aboard and set sail."],[592.04,"So they're working their way down."],[593.32,"We've talked before about the fact that at this point in history, you didn't just sort of sail across the Mediterranean."],[599.46,"If you're trying to go from Jerusalem to Athens, you didn't just do a straight shot."],[606.1,"They rarely went very far from the coast, if they could at all help it."],[610.98,"That's how they navigated."],[612.5,"So this is St."],[614.26,"Paul working his way down there."],[615.7,"Now remember, Ephesus is on the western coast of Asia Minor, what's now Turkey."],[620.5,"So they're going down around and across Phoenicia is what's now Lebanon."],[627.3,"So he's working his way down the coast toward Jerusalem is uh what this is communicating."],[635.62,"And finding a ship, oh sorry."],[638.18,"When we had uh sighte.d Cyprus, we passed it on the left, sailed to Syria, and landed at Tyre"],[645.9,"For there the ship was to unload her cargo."],[649.58,"So notice this is we again."],[652.14,"So St. Luke is is uh still with them."],[656.54,"Also notice it seems to be that you sort of find a ship that's going where you're going anyway, and pay for passage."],[663.74,"Uh-huh. Yeah. Yeah, they didn't have sort of passenger lines."],[670.7,"Yeah, so it was it was be but there was constant trade and cargo being shipped."],[677.42,"Excuse me. And so, yes, you'd you'd buy pa book passage, you know, uh."],[684.74,"Um and uh that was sometimes a way for a uh boat captain who was working for someone else to make a little money on the side was by taking some money to bring people on board."],[697.14,"Now that could sometimes be dangerous also because sometimes people like that would end up getting impressed into service."],[703.94,"Um it wasn't just the British Navy that did that."],[706.74,"Uh they would end up uh becoming sailors, not necessarily of their own free will."],[712.26,"Um but yeah, it was so you have to sort of find a boat going close."],[719.07,"So he says, We found a boat go heading towards Phoenicia."],[721.95,"Well, that's closer."],[722.83,"So so It's heading towards Lebanon."],[725.05,"So we'll take that, and then once we get there, we'll try and find a boat heading for Jerusalem."],[730.29,"Or at least closer, you know, somewhere further down the coast."],[734.61,"And uh Tyre, of course, is a city that uh at this point there was a land bridge."],[741.25,"Um Tyre uh was one of the capitals of uh Phoenicia in the ancient world."],[748.77,"Uh the other one was Sidon, which was nearby."],[751.65,"But uh Tyre was a city that was built entirely on an island originally, and the walls went all the way to the coast."],[760.13,"So it was considered to be completely unconquerable uh for most of ancient history, and then Alexander the Great conquered it in the fourth century BC."],[770.37,"Building a yeah, building essentially having uh a whole lot of his troops haul on rocks and impressed slave labor and building a causeway out to it so he could lay siege to the to the walls."],[785.53,"So at this point, it was connected to the shore."],[792.01,"It was still a major trade city, because of course, being along the coast, and since everyone has to sail up and down the coast, it became a major port and stop."],[800.73,"But uh at this point, the Phoenician Empire, which at one point was dominated trade, was the wealthiest financial empire in the ancient world, uh, had been wiped out by the Romans."],[815.53,"Uh after after Tyre uh was conquered by Alexander, yeah, by Alexander, the second largest city of the Phoenician Empire had been Carthage in North Africa."],[831.33,"And that sort of shifted to become the most major city since Tyre was gone."],[836.85,"And uh so that sort of became the capital, and then they got into it with Rome, while uh while Rome still was uh was uh a republic uh before they were an empire, but uh they got into a series of trade disputes that led to territorial disputes that led to anger, that led to three wars, um one of which the Romans nearly lost."],[864.81,"That's when Hannibal Hannibal was a Carthaginian general and managed to get he didn't get many actually into Italy, but uh managed to get at least a couple of elephants."],[877.05,"He went up through the Alps and came down into Italy."],[880.57,"Elephants are very poor mountain climbers."],[882.49,"Yeah, which is why not a lot of them made it."],[884.65,"Um but very came very close to conquering Italy."],[889.61,"Um they genuinely thought he might."],[892.25,"Uh but uh it eventually got to the point where I'm trying to remember which senator it was who ended every speech."],[900.41,"Um Cato the Younger."],[903.05,"Yeah it was Ca it was Cato, yeah."],[904.65,"Cato ended every speech he gave in the in the Senate with Carthage must Bero Dyested."],[909.21,"Didn't matter what he was talking about"],[911.49,"That was just his sign-off line."],[915.49,"You know, he could be talking about crop yields, he could be talking about building a road."],[919.33,"He did the he did his speech with Carthage Must Be Destroyed."],[923.25,"And so eventually uh Rome got around to it."],[926.77,"And in what's called the Third Punic War, Punic is sort of how they shortened Phoenician in Latin."],[932.61,"Um they wiped out uh Carthage and the surrounding area, and according to tradition, at least, although archaeology hasn't bored this out yet, uh, wet works salt into all the fields uh so that nothing could be grown there again."],[949.49,"Soil wouldn't be any good for growing crops, so they couldn't come back."],[953.17,"You know, exactly."],[953.73,"a And then after they did that, they built city there."],[954.13,"Rome had a habit of doing that though"],[960.97,"Um there was a time in Byzantine history where the capital of the Roman Empire was almost moved to Carthage."],[967.93,"Yeah, Caligula almost moved it to Alexandria."],[971.45,"Um, but then they killed him."],[974.17,"So uh but um so yeah, the Romans tended, we talked about that with Corinth when we talked about St."],[981.53,"Paul going to Corinth, that Corinth had been this important Greek city, and the Romans came and leveled it and built a new one just to sort of make a point to all the Greeks."],[989.61,"You know, this is this is what we do."],[992.57,"Your city will be next if you cause trouble."],[995.61,"Um they did that with a couple of cities and well, they ultimately did that with Jerusalem."],[1003.09,"In 132 AD, they did that to Jerusalem after the Barkokfa rebellion."],[1007.17,"They leveled it to the ground and built a city called Ilia Capitolina."],[1010.45,"Hadrian did on top of that."],[1013.73,"So that was sort of their way of doing things."],[1016.77,"Well, it is to say, Tyre, when we read about in the Old Testament, and we read the prophecies against Tyre and Ezekiel, that's talking about the Phoenicians."],[1025.17,"That's talking about this Phoenician Empire, uh, which was the center of Baal worship, which was always causing problems in the Old Testament."],[1033.65,"Um that's where Jezebel was from."],[1038.77,"Jezebel was the daughter of the king of Sidon, who, to make an alliance with Israel. got married off to Ahab and then caused all the trouble"],[1048.41,"So the Old Testament, you read a lot about Tyre and Sidon both because of that."],[1055.29,"At this point in history, though, it's just a Roman city because there are no more."],[1062.09,"There are probably people descended from Phoenicians, but there aren't any, there's no Phoenician Empire at this point in history."],[1079.77,"So at Tyre he finds that there's this little Christian community that started there."],[1085.33,"Um this was we we've seen this a few times in the book of Acts. where they sort of find some Christians somewhere"],[1093.89,"Um this is because remember, everybody got scattered after Saint Stephen was martyred."],[1099.33,"And we also had the people from Pentecost, who had come from all over the world, they hear about Jesus."],[1106.07,"Most of them then went home."],[1108.33,"So the gospel has kind of gotten out all kinds of places, and there are these little communities."],[1113.53,"And so sometimes Saint Paul is finding them."],[1116.81,"When we get, you know, the next book we're gonna read is the book of Romans."],[1120.81,"Uh, when Saint Paul wrote the epistle to the Romans, he had never been to Rome at that point."],[1129.77,"That's and either in St."],[1131.77,"Peter. There was already a church there there because Christians had gone."],[1133.21,"See, I had once had the impression that if St"],[1139.97,"Paul hadn't come and started a church, there wasn't a church."],[1142.93,"No, not that's not always the case."],[1144.85,"Yeah. Now it's we've seen in some cases that was the case."],[1148.13,"There are churches that were founded by St."],[1149.73,"Paul and his missionary journeys."],[1151.17,"But there are other places, especially places closer to Jerusalem."],[1156.87,"Like Tyre, like Antioch, there were already Christians there when he went there."],[1161.91,"Um, these cities that are near Jerusalem and that have of large Jewish communities, a lot of them got the gospel sort of more naturally."],[1170.15,"And remember, the other apostles, even though we aren't reading about them, this is the other thing."],[1175.19,"You know, we when we started out the book of Acts, there was a lot of stuff, St."],[1178.07,"of And then we've sort been following Saint Paul now."],[1178.99,"Peter, St. John, you know, Saint James."],[1184.84,"But don't let that deceive you into thinking that the other ones are just sitting around eating grapes in Jerusalem or something."],[1192.3,"I mean, they've been witnessing preaching, traveling."],[1196.64,"Remember the last time we saw St."],[1198.0,"Peter, he had gone down to Joppa."],[1200.08,"He had gone down."],[1201.12,"So they're traveling too."],[1202.64,"St. Philip, we saw, went and evangelized Samaria."],[1207.3,"And so, you know, they're they're also preaching the gospel."],[1212.1,"It's just, you know, Saint Luke is following one because he's one story arc."],[1218.04,"Yeah, he's following one story arc."],[1219.64,"He knows that story."],[1220.52,"But there are a whole lot. of other stories, you know, that could have been written"],[1224.88,"It's just the world could contain all the books if we wrote everything that every apostle did."],[1232.16,"Remember, there's not only the 12, there's the 70."],[1236.0,"So they find this Christian community there."],[1242.8,"And so Saint Paul is making good time."],[1245.93,"On his way to Jerusalem."],[1247.53,"And so they stop there for a week so they could spend time with the Christian community there."],[1253.69,"They told Paul through the Spirit not to go up to Jerusalem."],[1258.49,"So they have the same impression from the same place that St."],[1261.53,"Paul does, that if he goes to Jerusalem, this is going to be it."],[1264.67,"St. Paul, even though he's resolute about it, they're sort of like, hey, maybe you shouldn't go to Jerusalem because bad things are going to happen when you go there."],[1278.15,"When we had come to the end of those days, we departed and went on our way, and they all accompanied us with wives and children till we were out of the city."],[1287.83,"And we knelt down on the shore and prayed."],[1290.79,"When we had taken our leave of one another, we boarded the ship and they returned home."],[1296.63,"So what we see is not only that there's a Christian community there, but that this is a loving and faithful community."],[1303.51,"You know, this is this is a community that has its own, you know, life entire."],[1309.17,"Even though um even though we don't read the story of the apostle who went there and and put the flag down."],[1317.15,"Um so this is another a a reminder not only that the other apostles were doing things, but just because we haven't read about a city."],[1326.45,"Doesn't mean there are Christians there, there isn't a church there, that the Holy Spirit isn't working there, that it's not important."],[1332.64,"Again we're only following one through line."],[1334.9,"So even the church in Lafayette, Louisiana is important."],[1341.7,"Yeah, not terribly old and not terrible."],[1343.58,"Well, this this church isn't terribly old either."],[1345.7,"That's what I meant."],[1346.58,"Yeah. No, I mean this one, entire."],[1349.22,"Yeah, no I guarantee you it's not more than twenty years old."],[1352.74,"Yeah. And when we had finished our voyage from Tyre, we came to Ptolemaeus, greeted the brethren, and stayed with them one day."],[1365.82,"And the next day, we who are Paul's companions, so that makes it even more specific."],[1369.82,"It's not just a vague we, it's we who are Paul's companions."],[1372.94,"So St. Luke is including himself."],[1377.02,"Yep. Departed and came to Caesarea and entered the house of Philip the Evangelist."],[1386.7,"Who was one of the seven and stayed with him."],[1389.18,"Now, one of the seven refers to, remember, the deacons."],[1392.3,"There were seven deacons."],[1393.26,"This is St."],[1393.82,"Philip the Deacon."],[1395.74,"Why is he called Philip the Evangelist?"],[1398.34,"Well, remember, he's the one who went and preached in Samaria and preached to the Ethiopian eunuch."],[1403.78,"It's this Saint Philip who did that."],[1406.98,"So this is him coming back up."],[1408.92,"Now, again, this is another example."],[1410.94,"We don't read about how he came to Caesarea."],[1413.26,"We don't read about what he's been doing in between, but he's been evangelizing."],[1418.62,"He's been preaching."],[1422.14,"Now, this man had four virgin daughters who prophesied."],[1426.46,"Wait, is that Philip?"],[1428.14,"Yes. Okay. Yes."],[1430.34,"Um Virgin Dhaugters is technically correct, but the point is that they're young and unmarried."],[1436.94,"Uh-huh That's the it's not really about sexuality in this case."],[1442.3,"Um they're young, unmarried daughters"],[1449.62,"And as we stayed many days, a certain prophet named Agabas came down from Judea."],[1456.02,"Remember Agabas? He popped up once before."],[1458.82,"Yeah. In Antioch."],[1460.98,"Yeah, when Saint Paul was first in Antioch, Agabas showed up and warned that there was gonna be a famine."],[1467.22,"And remember, because of his warning that there was gonna be a famine, they uh they went and sort of made provisions."],[1476.18,"Um for the Christian communities that were gonna be affected."],[1480.58,"So now he comes again."],[1481.94,"He comes from Judea over to uh Caesarea to see Saint Paul."],[1488.22,"When he had come to us, he took Paul's belt, bound his own hands and feet, and says, Thus says the Holy Spirit, so shall the Jews at Jerusalem bind the man who owns this belt and deliver him into the hands of the Gentiles."],[1504.86,"So just in case you weren't clear."],[1509.38,"St. Paul's already said that he knows this."],[1511.38,"We saw that Christians entire said, Hey, this is what's gonna happen."],[1514.58,"Now Agabas is like him was the first one."],[1519.94,"No, this is this is just Agabas."],[1521.86,"Okay. Is Agabus a Christian?"],[1524.42,"Yes. Yeah. Yeah, he was in Antioch."],[1530.02,"It never specifically says, but when he c he was in Antioch and he made the prophecy about the famine, it didn't say that he came from somewhere else."],[1531.78,"Uh-huh So the presumption would be that he was part of the church in Antioch, but it never specifically says that."],[1548.54,"And apparently at this point he was in Judea somewhere."],[1551.02,"And Antioch is not in Judea."],[1552.64,"And Liban these days they uh they have the name a lot."],[1557.12,"Uh Agabias. It is very known there."],[1560.16,"Oh yeah. Yeah."],[1563.2,"That's probably because of the connection of him to Antioch."],[1566.0,"Yeah. When we had heard these things."],[1575.52,"Both we and those from that place pleaded with him not to go up to Jerusalem."],[1582.68,"So everybody says, okay, you know, uh say Paul, are you not getting this?"],[1588.36,"Like everybody's telling you if you go there, it's gonna be bad."],[1591.0,"Why are you still trying to get there and why are you in a hurry?"],[1593.4,"You know? They say, please don't do it."],[1596.6,"And Paul answered, What do you mean by weeping and breaking my heart?"],[1601.8,"For I'm ready not only to be bound, but also to die in Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus."],[1609.4,"So again, St."],[1610.84,"Luke is setting up this parallel."],[1612.4,"Remember when Saint Peter said to Jesus, you know, Oh no, we'll never let them."],[1619.16,"And he said, Get behind me, Satan."],[1620.6,"So showing that St"],[1624.0,"Paul has this same willingness that Christ had to lay his life down if need be."],[1630.88,"So when he would not be persuaded, we cease to saying the will of the Lord be done."],[1639.04,"So eventually they just stop and say, Okay, well, whatever God's will is, that's what'll happen."],[1646.48,"And after those days we packed and went up to Jerusalem."],[1649.76,"Also, some of the disciples from Caesarea went with us and brought with them a certain this is pronounced Nason M N A S O N that looks like it needs to buy another vowel or two, but that's pronounced Nason of Cyprus, an early disciple with whom we were to lodge."],[1671.8,"Okay. So Nason basically it means remembrance in Greek."],[1675.8,"That's what his name means."],[1678.04,"I actually had a friend in college with that name whose parents named him that from the Bible church."],[1684.04,"I was sort of like, really?"],[1687.64,"Um and so notice it from Cyprus, early disciple."],[1691.4,"Remember the very first place that St."],[1694.28,"Paul and St."],[1694.84,"Barnabas went on the first missionary journey was to Cyprus."],[1697.8,"So that's what it means when it says an early disciple."],[1700.12,"This is somebody from that original group who knows uh St."],[1705.48,"Paul, and so they're staying with him."],[1710.6,"And when we had come to Jerusalem, the brethren received us gladly"],[1715.12,"On the following day, Paul went in with us to James, and all the elders were present."],[1722.32,"We're translated elders there as presbyters."],[1728.4,"So this is, you know, not just Saint James, the brother of the Lord, who, as we said, is has this leadership position, his position is bishop of the church of Jerusalem, but also the other apostles who are there in town, and then his presbyters who serve with him in Jerusalem."],[1748.0,"So first he comes to Jerusalem, he's received by the people of the church, and then he goes on the next day to visit Saint James."],[1757.48,"When he had greeted them, he told in detail those things which God had done among the Gentiles through his ministry."],[1764.2,"And when they heard it, they glorified the Lord, and they said to him, You see, brother, how many myriads of Jews there are who have believed, and they are all zealous for the law."],[1777.08,"But they have been informed about you that you teach all the Jews who are among the Gentiles to forsake Moses, saying that they ought not to circumcise their children, nor to walk according to the customs."],[1788.36,"So St Paul comes, he tells them what's been going on about all the Christian communities that have been springing up, all the people who've been coming to believe in Jesus, both Jews and Gentiles, how places he's gone."],[1800.76,"They're happy about that."],[1801.48,"But then St"],[1804.16,"James says, but."],[1807.17,"But there's a certain group who have been spreading this rumor about you."],[1816.27,"Which we know isn't true."],[1817.39,"We've seen isn't true."],[1818.93,"That's saying you're going to all these synagogues and you're going to all these Jewish communities, all these places, and telling them not to follow the law anymore, not to circumcise their children, not to follow the traditions of our people."],[1833.25,"Now we've seen that's not what he's saying."],[1835.9,"In fact, quite the opposite."],[1837.18,"Remember, he had St."],[1838.46,"Timothy be circumcised, sort of expressly to make that point."],[1845.24,"And what he's actually, as we saw when we talked about Acts 15 in the Council of Jerusalem, what he's actually been saying is that the Gentiles can become part of the Christian community without going through the Jewish rituals."],[1862.68,"They don't have to become Jewish and then become Christians."],[1865.54,"They can just become Christians."],[1866.98,"So he's telling the Gentiles they don't have to be circumcised in order to become Christians."],[1871.86,"They don't have to keep the kosher rules."],[1873.78,"They don't have to."],[1875.14,"Nowhere have we seen, in fact, we've seen the opposite several times, where he's telling Jewish people to stop doing it."],[1881.88,"Because they believe in Jesus."],[1883.56,"And so this is an important distinction."],[1885.66,"So this accusation against St."],[1887.1,"Paul isn't true."],[1887.26,"isn't true. It"],[1890.58,"He isn't going around saying, toss all that old stuff, forget about Moses."],[1895.63,"Forget about the old testament."],[1897.71,"In his homies, all the time he started saying, I'm not he, um, I'm here to fill it out, you know."],[1904.03,"I'm not to here to cancel."],[1905.85,"To fulfill. Every time we've seen him talking in the synagogue to Jews, he talks about Abraham and he talks about these are the traditions of our father."],[1914.51,"And that Jesus is the what he's preaching, is that Jesus is the Messiah."],[1918.69,"That he's the Jewish Messiah."],[1920.69,"He's the king of the Jews."],[1922.07,"Descended from David."],[1923.67,"Said, I came not to abolish the old ball, but to fulfill it."],[1927.83,"Yeah. Yeah. So this is a this is a false accusation."],[1932.96,"But it's getting people stirred up."],[1936.18,"It's getting people stirred up."],[1939.46,"What then? The assembly must certainly meet, for they will hear that you have come."],[1946.58,"Therefore, do what we tell you."],[1948.26,"We have four men who have taken a vow."],[1951.22,"Take them and be purified with them, and pay their expenses, so that they may shave their heads, and that all may know that those things of which they were informed concerning you are nothing, but that you yourself also walk orderly and keep the law."],[1965.22,"w So we talked about this vow taking, the Nazarite vo a little bit once before, because remember St."],[1965.86,"Paul took one"],[1972.94,"That was another example of how he had he himself in his old life had to set aside these things."],[1981.42,"This is sort of the ancestor of our monastic vows."],[1986.3,"Famously, St. John the Forerunner was a Nazirite."],[1989.1,"That was one of the things that Archangel Gabriel said to his father was that he would be a Nazirite from his mother's womb."],[1996.94,"And uh we also talked about how Samson was supposed to be a Nazirite, but sort of broke all those vows, one after the other, in various ways."],[2007.74,"But uh among the vows, you didn't drink any alcohol, you didn't uh touch anything unclean, especially dead bodies and other things of that nature."],[2019.58,"So you kept yourself ritually clean and pure."],[2024.98,"And you uh didn't cut your hair or your beard."],[2031.64,"And so when they began that vow as a sign that they had taken that vow, they would shave their head completely."],[2039.16,"And then just let it grow from that point."],[2042.34,"And not and not cut it."],[2043.78,"And sometimes that vow was taken permanently, like with St."],[2046.5,"John the Forerunner."],[2047.7,"Sometimes that was taken for a time."],[2050.34,"If there was something you were praying for or something you were dedicating yourself to, you'd do it for a period of time."],[2058.78,"Um, but in either case, that's that's what we're talking about."],[2062.52,"That's the kind of vow we're talking about."],[2064.32,"It's sort of a way of setting yourself and your time, at least for a period, aside for God."],[2071.6,"And so, St."],[2072.72,"James says, Look, we've got four men who are about to take these vows."],[2077.52,"They're gonna go over to the temple and and take their vows, they're gonna shave their heads."],[2081.68,"He says, Here's what you should do."],[2084.92,"Go with them, let everybody see you."],[2088.46,"Going with them, pay for their expenses, to get their heads shaved and everything, and and everything they're gonna do."],[2095.44,"That way, everyone will know, they'll say, Oh, well, hey, I heard he, you know, isn't he the traditional?"],[2101.2,"But look, he's there, sort of sponsoring these four people who are taking this vow."],[2104.96,"Clearly, he believes in the traditions of our fathers but hasn't he already done this so when he did it before was that for a time because what I'm driving at is uh if he's shaved his head once and he's not supposed to cut it again then shaving it again would be against him doing it oh this is these four people are going to do it right four he's gonna sponsor them okay to do it but he's not gonna do that but do that publicly so that's not gonna do that right so that his name is attached to it."],[2139.28,"So that people will see, oh, well, look, if he didn't believe in our traditions, why would he be sponsoring these four okay people?"],[2145.36,"He's encouraging them."],[2150.72,"Okay. But concerning the Gentiles who believe, we have written and decided that they should observe no such thing, except that they should keep themselves from things offered to idols, from blood, from things strangled, and from sexual immorality."],[2165.52,"So calling us back to Acts chapter 15."],[2170.0,"So St. Luke has that here, so we remember."],[2173.86,"This isn't contradictory again."],[2176.98,"This is the same position that's been all the way through."],[2180.28,"That those things, and we we talked about that when we talked about Acts 15."],[2184.04,"That the vast majority of the law, when you read it, you just read it in the original context, is given to the people of Israel."],[2194.68,"And it's specifically given to them as a way for them to be different than all the other nations of the world."],[2201.28,"God never says, I want you to go and conquer your neighbors and force them not to eat pork."],[2211.4,"Our Muslim friends do that."],[2213.64,"But he never told the Jewish people to go do that."],[2217.24,"He never told the Jewish people, you need to go and force these people to keep the Sabbath."],[2222.86,"You need to go and force these other nations to not wear cloth made of or garments made of two different types of cloth."],[2231.18,"He never said that."],[2233.63,"And we talked about specifically in Exodus, Exodus chapters 17 through 21."],[2238.19,"It's called the Holiness Code."],[2239.07,"And there it's very specific"],[2242.23,"All these things are for Israel to be holy and set apart and different, except for four things."],[2249.91,"There's four things, and really two of them are pretty much the same thing."],[2255.13,"Where it says, you can't do this, and if there are foreigners staying with you, they can't do this."],[2261.71,"No one can do this."],[2263.67,"And those those things are idolatry."],[2268.25,"Worshipping idols. Then here in Acts, remember there was eating blood and eating meat from strangled animals."],[2276.89,"And the problem with meat from strangled animals is the blood's still in there."],[2280.57,"So those are both eating blood."],[2282.29,"So in Leviticus, it's idolatry, eating blood, and sexual immorality."],[2287.35,"Those three things aren't allowed for anybody."],[2290.07,"And so when the issue came up in the book of Acts in the early church, they said, Well, we've got Jewish people here who are keeping all these Jewish traditions still."],[2299.27,"Now we've got these Gentiles here also coming into the church."],[2302.95,"Do they have to start doing all that?"],[2307.16,"What they did was they didn't scrap the law, the Torah."],[2312.04,"They went back to Leviticus and read it and said, Well, no, look."],[2316.01,"All of these things are just for the people of Israel, but these things."],[2320.34,"Idolatry, sexual immorality, eating blood, nobody can do."],[2323.94,"And so they issued, they just restated what Leviticus said."],[2328.6,"They said, the Gentiles who are coming into the church, they're coming into the God's people."],[2334.33,"They're like the aliens, the strangers, the foreigners in Leviticus, they can't worship idols and be in the church."],[2341.29,"They can't eat blood and be in the church."],[2343.69,"They can't be sexually immoral and be in the church."],[2347.21,"So those three things they have to stop."],[2349.69,"But other than that, these other things."],[2352.27,"The law never said they have to follow."],[2356.75,"And so that's sort of reiterated for us here."],[2360.03,"A lot of a lot of modern folks try and make this a big contradiction."],[2367.87,"They try and say, oh, well, look, St"],[2371.89,"Paul's saying the Gentiles don't have to do this stuff, but then he's going along with it here."],[2376.26,"And that's because that's not what he's saying."],[2380.48,"He's saying exactly what the Old Testament said."],[2384.56,"Exactly what the Old Testament said."],[2385.84,"And that's not that all this stuff is tossed."],[2388.46,"It still applies, it still applies the same way it always applied."],[2391.68,"There's Jews and there's Gentiles, and they're now all in the church."],[2397.36,"So this is the plan, at least."],[2398.8,"The plan, at least, is for St."],[2400.24,"Paul to go to the temple with these four men, sponsor them, and thereby show he still believes in the law and the traditions of the Jewish people."],[2412.84,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2420.2,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2425.56,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.0,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: three journeys, Antioch as base","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>Acts 21</b>; first Acts study (intro to the book) free online</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul has completed <b>three missionary journeys</b> - maps in the back of some Bibles</li><li class=\"d1\">whether there was a <b>fourth</b> = conjecture; \"we'll get to that\" at end of book</li><li class=\"d0\">Patterns seen:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Antioch</b> as home base; always returning there</li><li class=\"d1\">route: central <b>Asia Minor</b> → up into <b>Macedonia</b> → down into <b>Greece</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why he and <b>Peter</b> are patron saints of the <b>Patriarchate of Antioch</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Also: going back to <b>Jerusalem for the feasts</b> whenever able</li></ul>"},{"t":152.28,"lv":3,"h":"Not a brand-new religion","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Some Protestant friends: once Christ came, <b>Judaism over</b>, \"all scrapped\", start from scratch</li><li class=\"d1\">worship totally different, \"meeting in houses\", \"nothing on the walls\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Acts shows the opposite</li><li class=\"d1\">early chapters: apostles still going to <b>temple to pray</b></li><li class=\"d1\">celebrating the feasts; Paul on a long hard journey just to reach Jerusalem for them</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul in synagogues, preaching from the <b>Old Testament</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Apostles saw it as <b>continuation and fulfillment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ fulfilled the prophecies → what we do is \"<b>greater and better</b>\", but the continuation</li></ul>"},{"t":264.75,"lv":3,"h":"Pascha and Pentecost as fulfilled feasts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul at Jerusalem for <b>Pascha</b> - not celebrating the Exodus</li><li class=\"d1\">celebrating the anniversary of <b>Christ's resurrection</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now coming for <b>Pentecost</b> - not the giving of the law at Sinai</li><li class=\"d1\">celebrating the <b>coming of the Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Cycle of feasts \"taken over as a fulfillment\" - apostles already doing it right after the events</li><li class=\"d0\">Most of the NT not yet written</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul maybe wrote <b>Galatians</b>, maybe <b>1 Thessalonians</b>; Romans \"debatable\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Shape of the church visible in worship and preaching: things <b>transformed, bigger, more important</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Matters because chapter 21 is about to hinge on this</li></ul>"},{"t":357.92,"lv":2,"h":"Where we left off: Ephesus farewell","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul heading for <b>Jerusalem for Pentecost</b>; stopped at <b>Ephesus</b> to say farewell to the <b>presbyters</b> he had placed</li><li class=\"d0\">He taught them:</li><li class=\"d1\">what <b>leadership in the church</b> means</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>example</b> he set among them - keep doing the same</li><li class=\"d1\">convinced by the <b>Holy Spirit</b> they'd not see him in the flesh again</li><li class=\"d0\">Also not just an impression - real opposition</li><li class=\"d1\">Jews who rejected Jesus following city to city, trying to get him in trouble with <b>Rome</b>, killed</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>flogged, beaten, imprisoned, stoned</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now going to the center of that world - a community he was once part of</li><li class=\"d1\">\"out there persecuting Christians too\"</li></ul>"},{"t":469.24,"lv":3,"h":"Luke's parallel: Paul and Jesus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Today is <b>St. Luke's feast</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel of Luke + Acts = <b>one two-volume work</b>, \"Acts isn't a sequel\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Acts 1: previous book = all Jesus <b>began</b> to do and teach</li><li class=\"d1\">so in Acts, Jesus <b>continues</b> working and teaching through the apostles</li><li class=\"d0\">Deliberate parallel: Paul going up to Jerusalem ~ <b>Jesus going up</b> in Luke</li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus knew exactly what would happen, predicted it, still <b>resolute</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>follows Christ</b> in willingness to suffer and die for the gospel</li></ul>"},{"t":569.24,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 21:1-3 - Coasting down to Tyre","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Kos</b>, <b>Rhodes</b>, <b>Patara</b>; ship for <b>Phoenicia</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient sailing: no straight shot across the Mediterranean</li><li class=\"d1\">\"rarely went very far from the coast\" - how they navigated</li><li class=\"d1\">Ephesus on west coast of Asia Minor (now <b>Turkey</b>); Phoenicia = now <b>Lebanon</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Passed <b>Cyprus</b> on the left, sailed to <b>Syria</b>, landed at <b>Tyre</b> - ship to unload cargo</li><li class=\"d0\">Still \"<b>we</b>\" - <b>Luke</b> still with them</li><li class=\"d0\">No passenger lines: find a cargo ship going your way, <b>buy passage</b></li><li class=\"d1\">captain earning a little on the side</li><li class=\"d1\">danger: could be <b>impressed into service</b> - \"wasn't just the British Navy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Plan: boat toward Lebanon, then find one further down the coast</li></ul>"},{"t":734.61,"lv":3,"h":"Tyre and the fall of Phoenicia","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Tyre</b> = capital of <b>Phoenicia</b> (other: <b>Sidon</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">originally built on an <b>island</b>, walls to the coast - \"unconquerable\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Alexander the Great</b>, 4th c. BC: troops hauling rocks, slave labor → <b>causeway</b> for siege</li><li class=\"d1\">now connected to shore; still a major port on the coast route</li><li class=\"d0\">Phoenician Empire - once the wealthiest, dominated trade - <b>wiped out by Rome</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Next city: <b>Carthage</b> in North Africa</li><li class=\"d1\">trade disputes → territorial disputes → anger → <b>three wars</b>, one Rome nearly lost</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hannibal</b> over the Alps - \"elephants are very poor mountain climbers\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Cato</b> ended every speech \"<b>Carthage must be destroyed</b>\" - crop yields or roads, didn't matter</li></ul>"},{"t":923.25,"lv":3,"h":"Third Punic War and Rome's habits","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Third Punic War</b>: Punic = Latin shortening of Phoenician</li><li class=\"d1\">Carthage wiped out; tradition: <b>salt in the fields</b> (archaeology hasn't borne it out)</li><li class=\"d1\">then Rome built a city there; later Byzantines nearly moved the capital to Carthage; <b>Caligula</b> nearly to Alexandria - \"then they killed him\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans' pattern: level a city and rebuild it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Corinth</b> - a point made to all the Greeks: \"your city will be next\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jerusalem</b>, <b>132 AD</b> after the <b>Bar Kokhba</b> rebellion → <i>Aelia Capitolina</i> (Hadrian)</li></ul>"},{"t":1016.77,"lv":3,"h":"Tyre in the Old Testament","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Prophecies against Tyre in <b>Ezekiel</b> = about the Phoenician Empire</li><li class=\"d0\">Center of <b>Baal worship</b> - always causing problems</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jezebel</b>: daughter of the king of <b>Sidon</b>, married off to <b>Ahab</b> for an alliance</li><li class=\"d1\">hence Tyre and Sidon both in OT</li><li class=\"d0\">By Paul's day: \"just a Roman city\"; maybe descendants, but no Phoenician empire</li></ul>"},{"t":1079.77,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 21:4-6 - A Christian community at Tyre","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Finds a small Christian community already there - seen a few times in Acts</li><li class=\"d1\">after <b>Stephen's martyrdom</b> everyone scattered; <b>Pentecost</b> pilgrims went home with the gospel</li><li class=\"d0\">Next book: <b>Romans</b> - Paul had <b>never been to Rome</b>, church already there</li><li class=\"d1\">not always Paul founding churches; near Jerusalem (<b>Tyre</b>, <b>Antioch</b>) Christians were already there</li><li class=\"d0\">Remember the other apostles: \"not sitting around eating grapes in Jerusalem\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Peter</b> down to Joppa, <b>Philip</b> evangelized Samaria; also the <b>70</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Luke follows <b>one story arc</b> - \"the world could contain all the books\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Stay <b>a week</b>; they tell Paul <b>through the Spirit</b> not to go up</li><li class=\"d1\">same impression as Paul: \"bad things are going to happen\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1278.15,"lv":3,"h":"Farewell on the shore","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Left after the days; all accompany them with <b>wives and children</b> out of the city</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Knelt on the shore and prayed</b>, took leave; they returned home</li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>loving, faithful community</b> with its \"life entire\"</li><li class=\"d1\">we never read who planted it</li><li class=\"d0\">Reminder: no story about a city ≠ no church there, no Spirit working, not important</li><li class=\"d1\">\"even the church in Lafayette, Louisiana is important\" - and this one \"not more than twenty years old\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1352.74,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 21:7-9 - Ptolemais, Caesarea, Philip","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Voyage from Tyre ends at <b>Ptolemais</b>; greet brethren, one day</li><li class=\"d0\">Next day to <b>Caesarea</b> - \"we who are Paul's companions\" → <b>Luke</b> includes himself</li><li class=\"d0\">Stay in house of <b>Philip the Evangelist</b>, \"one of the seven\" = the <b>deacons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same Philip who preached in <b>Samaria</b> and to the <b>Ethiopian eunuch</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no account of how he came to Caesarea - but he's been evangelizing</li><li class=\"d0\">Four <b>virgin daughters who prophesied</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"virgin\" correct, but point is <b>young and unmarried</b>; \"not really about sexuality\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1449.62,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 21:10-11 - Agabus's prophecy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Stayed many days; prophet <b>Agabus</b> comes down from Judea</li><li class=\"d0\">Appeared once before: <b>Antioch</b>, warned of <b>famine</b> → provisions for affected communities</li><li class=\"d0\">Takes <b>Paul's belt</b>, binds own hands and feet</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Thus says the Holy Spirit\": Jews at Jerusalem will <b>bind the owner of the belt</b> and deliver him to the <b>Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not new - Paul already knew; Christians at Tyre said it too</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Is Agabus a Christian? A: yes - presumably part of the church in <b>Antioch</b>, never stated</li><li class=\"d1\">Agabus still a common name in <b>Lebanon</b> - probably from the Antioch connection</li></ul>"},{"t":1566.0,"lv":3,"h":"Acts 21:12-14 - \"I am ready to die\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>We</b> and the locals plead: don't go up</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Paul, are you not getting this?\" - everyone says it'll be bad</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: \"What do you mean by <b>weeping and breaking my heart</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">ready not only to be <b>bound</b> but to <b>die in Jerusalem</b> for the name of the Lord Jesus</li><li class=\"d0\">Luke's parallel again: <b>Peter</b> \"we'll never let them\" → \"<b>Get behind me, Satan</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul has Christ's willingness to <b>lay down his life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not persuaded → \"<b>the will of the Lord be done</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1646.48,"lv":3,"h":"Acts 21:15-16 - Mnason of Cyprus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Pack up, go to Jerusalem; disciples from Caesarea come along</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mnason</b> of Cyprus, \"an early disciple\" - host for lodging</li><li class=\"d1\">name = <b>remembrance</b> in Greek; \"a friend in college with that name\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Cyprus</b> = first stop of Paul and <b>Barnabas</b> on the first journey</li><li class=\"d1\">\"early disciple\" = from that original group who knows Paul</li></ul>"},{"t":1710.6,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 21:17-19 - Received by James and the elders","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Brethren receive them gladly</li><li class=\"d0\">Next day to <b>James</b>, all the <b>elders</b> present (<i>presbyters</i>)</li><li class=\"d1\">James the brother of the Lord = <b>bishop of Jerusalem</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also the other apostles in town + his presbyters</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul reports in detail what God did among the <b>Gentiles</b> through his ministry</li><li class=\"d1\">all the communities, Jews and Gentiles coming to believe</li><li class=\"d0\">They <b>glorified the Lord</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1764.2,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 21:20-21 - The rumor against Paul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"How many <b>myriads of Jews</b> have believed\" - all <b>zealous for the law</b></li><li class=\"d0\">James's \"but\": rumor that Paul teaches Jews among Gentiles to <b>forsake Moses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't circumcise children, don't walk by the <b>customs</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"We've seen it isn't true\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul had <b>Timothy circumcised</b> expressly to make the point</li><li class=\"d0\">Getting people <b>stirred up</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1845.24,"lv":3,"h":"What Paul actually taught","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts 15</b>, Council of Jerusalem: <b>Gentiles</b> can become Christians without the Jewish rituals</li><li class=\"d1\">no circumcision, no <b>kosher</b> rules; \"don't have to become Jewish and then Christian\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Never telling <b>Jews</b> to stop - \"quite the opposite\", several times</li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"toss all that old stuff, forget Moses\"</li><li class=\"d1\">in synagogues: <b>Abraham</b>, \"traditions of our fathers\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus = the <b>Jewish Messiah</b>, king of the Jews, descended from <b>David</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I came not to abolish but to <b>fulfill</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Accusation false - but \"getting people stirred up\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1939.46,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 21:22-24 - James's plan: sponsor four men","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"The assembly must certainly meet, for they will hear you have come\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Four men have taken a vow → Paul to be <b>purified with them</b>, <b>pay their expenses</b> for shaving heads</li><li class=\"d1\">so all know the rumors are nothing: he \"<b>walks orderly and keeps the law</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul himself took a vow once - he too set these aside in his old life</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: hadn't Paul already shaved his head? A: no - he's <b>sponsoring the four</b>, publicly, his name attached</li><li class=\"d1\">\"if he didn't believe in our traditions, why sponsor these four?\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1965.22,"lv":3,"h":"The Nazirite vow","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ancestor of our <b>monastic vows</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>John the Forerunner</b> = Nazirite; Gabriel told his father: from his mother's womb</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Samson</b> supposed to be one - \"broke all those vows\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Rules:</li><li class=\"d1\">no <b>alcohol</b>; nothing unclean, esp. <b>dead bodies</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no cutting <b>hair or beard</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>shave the head completely</b> at start as sign, then let it grow</li><li class=\"d0\">Permanent (John) or <b>for a time</b> - praying for something or dedicating yourself</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Setting yourself and your time aside for God\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2150.72,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 21:25 - The Gentile decree restated","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Concerning the Gentiles... we have written\": <b>idols</b>, <b>blood</b>, <b>things strangled</b>, <b>sexual immorality</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Back to <b>Acts 15</b> - same position throughout, no contradiction</li><li class=\"d0\">Most of the law given to <b>Israel</b>, to make them <b>different</b> from other nations</li><li class=\"d1\">God never said conquer neighbors and force them off pork - \"our Muslim friends do that\"</li><li class=\"d1\">nor force Sabbath, nor ban <b>mixed-fiber garments</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2233.63,"lv":3,"h":"Holiness Code: three things for everyone","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Holiness Code</b> (Lev. 17-21) - all for Israel to be set apart, <b>except four things</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"two of them pretty much the same thing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">bind foreigners staying with you too: <b>idolatry</b>, <b>eating blood</b>, <b>sexual immorality</b></li><li class=\"d1\">strangled meat still has the blood → both = eating blood</li><li class=\"d0\">Early church: didn't scrap the <b>Torah</b> - went back to Leviticus</li><li class=\"d1\">Gentiles = like the <b>aliens, strangers</b> in Leviticus</li><li class=\"d1\">can't worship idols, eat blood, or be sexually immoral and be in the church</li><li class=\"d1\">the rest the law \"never said they have to follow\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2360.03,"lv":3,"h":"Not a contradiction","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Modern folks: Paul says Gentiles needn't, yet he goes along here → contradiction</li><li class=\"d0\">Not what he's saying - exactly what the <b>OT said</b></li><li class=\"d1\">law not tossed; applies as always</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jews and Gentiles</b> now all in the church</li><li class=\"d0\">The plan: Paul to the temple with the four men, <b>sponsor</b> them</li><li class=\"d1\">shows he still believes in the <b>law</b> and the traditions of the Jewish people</li></ul>"}]});
